<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Miss Wheatley, 19, comes for her preventive visit three weeks after her asthma review and about ten days before she sails abroad, where she expects to spend the summer while her book is printed. Her asthma is well controlled on as-needed budesonide-formoterol: she uses one inhalation about twice a week, has not woken at night for two months, and her peak flow stays between 360 and 400 L/min. She carries her action plan, her standby prednisone and her seasickness tablets. She asked for time to talk about contraception and about what asthma would mean in a future pregnancy, and was seen alone for the whole visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic preventive visit, age 19</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Asthma, mild persistent, well controlled on GINA track 1 step 2</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Budesonide-formoterol as needed; standby prednisone and meclizine for the journey</td></tr><tr><td>Upcoming</td><td>A long sea crossing and a summer away from her clinic</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on paper in a private room and reviewed with her alone by Dr. Hartley-Okonkwo. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good, and better than in the winter</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Her faith, her writing and a close friend she writes to; lives in the household described in her social history; hopes for her freedom</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco and exposures</td><td>Never smoked or vaped; now opens a window and uses the kitchen fan when cooking</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol and drugs</td><td>None (AUDIT-C 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks most days; no longer stops on the hills</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Three small meals; dairy most days; no restriction or bingeing</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Six to seven hours; writes late at night</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>Not sexually active; regular periods; no pregnancy now; she may wish to marry and have children one day</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Not hit, hurt or threatened; does not hold her own papers or wages (see social history); no firearm in the house</td></tr><tr><td>Road and travel safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; travel insurance and a copy of her medical summary arranged for the journey</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

