<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Periodic preventive visit completed</content>. Health risk assessment reviewed and a written prevention plan given. Depression and anxiety screens are minimal; no safety concern needing urgent action. [S]<list><item>Next preventive visit in one year.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma, well controlled (J45.30)</content>. Controlled on as-needed budesonide-formoterol; ready for the journey. [S]<list><item>Travel plan from the six-week review confirmed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Contraception and pregnancy planning</content>. Not sexually active and does not want contraception now. Every method, including the implant, intrauterine devices and combined pills, is suitable for a woman with asthma (U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria). If she becomes pregnant, well-controlled asthma is safe for her and the baby; uncontrolled asthma is the greater risk. Budesonide is the inhaled steroid with the most reassuring pregnancy data, and her controller should never be stopped because of a pregnancy. [S]<list><item>Folic acid 0.4 mg daily, by her choice.</item><item>Information on emergency contraception and on each method, to take with her.</item><item>Plan any pregnancy with the clinic: asthma review and spirometry before and during pregnancy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Screening tests for a woman born in West Africa</content>. Hepatitis B screening is recommended for people born where hepatitis B is common; HIV and hepatitis C once in adulthood; tuberculosis infection test already negative. [S]<list><item>Hepatitis B surface antigen, surface antibody and core antibody, HIV and hepatitis C antibody drawn today; results to her own phone.</item><item>Hepatitis B and HPV vaccine series started today.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Personalized prevention plan. A written copy was given to the patient. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Blood pressure screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>102/64 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Depression screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 score 4; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Anxiety screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>GAD-7 score 3</td></tr><tr><td>Unhealthy alcohol and drug use screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Intimate partner violence and exploitation screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Asked privately; no violence; the domestic servitude in her social history is known, and resources and social work have been offered and her choices respected</td></tr><tr><td>Contraception and pregnancy planning</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Counselled; no method wanted now; asthma in pregnancy discussed</td></tr><tr><td>Folic acid</td><td>Completed today</td><td>0.4 mg daily started, by her choice</td></tr><tr><td>Hepatitis B screening</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Born in a region where hepatitis B is common; antigen and antibodies drawn today</td></tr><tr><td>HIV and hepatitis C screening</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Once-in-adulthood tests drawn today with her agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Latent tuberculosis infection screening</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Interferon-gamma release assay negative at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Chlamydia and gonorrhea screening</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not sexually active; to be offered if that changes</td></tr><tr><td>Cervical cancer screening</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Begins at 21</td></tr><tr><td>HPV vaccine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dose 1 of 3 (series started after 15)</td></tr><tr><td>Hepatitis B vaccine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>HepB-CpG dose 1 of 2; dose 2 due in a month, after she returns</td></tr><tr><td>Pneumococcal vaccine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PCV20 two months ago (asthma is an indication from 19)</td></tr><tr><td>Influenza and COVID-19 vaccines</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Given this season two months ago; due again in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>Tetanus, diphtheria, and pertussis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Tdap at 12 on her school record, which also shows two doses each of MMR and varicella, polio and two MenACWY doses</td></tr><tr><td>Travel health</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No vaccines beyond the routine schedule are needed for the crossing and the summer in England; seasickness advice; insurance and a medical summary</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Human papillomavirus vaccine (9-valent), dose 1 of 3; Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 1 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); Medicare Preventive Services reference: visit templates, screening cascades, and documentation elements (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026); U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use, 2024 (2024); ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 90: Asthma in Pregnancy. Obstet Gynecol 2008;111(2 Pt 1):457-464 (PMID 18238988) (2008)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Results to her own phone. Hepatitis B and HPV doses after she returns. Asthma review on her return in the autumn, or sooner by message from abroad. Next preventive visit in one year. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-26: asthma review after an exacerbation with a cold on her return from abroad: control, repeat spirometry and FeNO, the winter plan, completion of vaccines, and her changed circumstances. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.21" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="42348-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Advance directives"/>
    <title>Advance Care Planning</title>
    <text><paragraph>Voluntary advance care planning discussion of about 16 minutes, with her consent. She chose to name a health care agent, a person she trusts outside the household, recorded in the confidential part of the chart and not named here, because she wants decisions about her care made by someone of her own choosing if she were ever unable to speak for herself, including while she is abroad. She wants full treatment for any illness she could recover from. She chose not to record other preferences today; to be revisited at any change in her health or circumstances. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>
